What I Delivered
Data Quality Assessment
The dataset had a subtle issue: the TotalCharges column contained empty strings disguised as valid data. Pandas doesn't catch these as missing values — they look like real data until you try to do math with them. My cleaning pipeline detected and handled this automatically.
Predictive Model
Built and compared three models:
ModelF1-ScoreROC-AUCWhat This MeansLogistic Regression0.6040.842Correctly distinguishes churners from non-churners 84% of the timeRandom Forest0.5760.836Slightly lower performance across all metricsXGBoost0.5670.829Most complex model, but no improvement over simpler ones
Why These Numbers Are Realistic (Not Inflated)
An F1 of 0.60 on churn prediction is honest and commercially viable. Most published solutions on this dataset land between 0.55-0.65. Here's why:

Churn is a human decision — inherently harder to predict than numerical patterns like fraud
The features are relatively simple — no call logs, no usage data, no customer service interactions
A model that catches 56% of churners while maintaining 66% precision means the retention team is reaching out to customers who genuinely are at risk, not wasting time on false alarms

Inflated numbers (0.95+ F1) on this dataset would indicate overfitting or data leakage, not a better model.
Key Findings (SHAP Explainability)
The SHAP analysis revealed the actual business drivers of churn, ranked by impact:

Contract type — Month-to-month customers churn at dramatically higher rates than those on annual contracts
Tenure — New customers (under 12 months) are the highest risk group
Monthly charges — Higher-paying customers churn more, suggesting perceived value issues
Internet service type — Fiber optic customers churn more than DSL (possibly higher cost or more competitive alternatives)
Tech support — Customers WITHOUT tech support churn at higher rates

Business Recommendations
FindingRecommended ActionExpected ImpactMonth-to-month contracts churn mostOffer incentives to convert to annual contracts (discount months 13-24)Reduce highest-risk segment by 20-30%New customers are highest riskImplement 90-day onboarding program with check-ins at days 7, 30, 60Catch problems before they drive churnHigh monthly charges correlate with churnAudit pricing vs perceived value; consider loyalty discounts at month 6Improve retention among high-revenue customersNo tech support → higher churnBundle tech support into base plans or offer free 3-month trialsRemove a key friction point at low costFiber optic customers churn moreInvestigate competitive landscape and service quality for fiber usersAddress potential service gap
This is what separates analysis from consulting — the model tells you what to DO, not just what happened.
